  "prediction": "The report outlines the Federal Emergency Management Agency's (FEMA) Fire Management Assistance Grants (FMAGs) program, which provides financial assistance to states and local governments for suppressing fires on non-federal lands. FMAGs can also cover costs related to evacuations, emergency work, and personal comfort items for firefighters. The program is not available for fires that begin on federal lands, except in certain circumstances where there is a cooperative agreement between the state and federal government for suppression activities. The report also mentions that hazard mitigation grants are now available for FMAG declarations, and the amount of assistance provided is based on the percentage of funds spent on FMAG assistance. The report emphasizes that FMAGs are intended to help prevent the need for a disaster declaration and that the Forest Service and other federal agencies offer various types of assistance related to wildfire management.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the Federal Emergency Management Agency's (FEMA) Fire Management Assistance Grants (FMAGs) program, which provides financial assistance to states and local governments for suppressing fires on non-federal lands. FMAGs can also cover costs related to evacuations, emergency work, and personal comfort items for firefighters. The program is not available for fires that begin on federal lands, except in certain circumstances where there is a cooperative agreement between the state and federal government for suppression activities. The report also mentions that hazard mitigation grants are now available for FMAG declarations, and the amount of assistance provided is based on the percentage of funds spent on FMAG assistance. The report emphasizes that FMAGs are intended to help prevent the need for a disaster declaration and that the Forest Service and other federal agencies offer various types of assistance related to wildfire management.",
  "score": 23.71133970639814,
  "answers": [
    "Section 420 of the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act (P.L. 93-288, hereinafter the Stafford Act) authorizes the President to \"declare\" a Fire Management Assistance Grant (FMAG). In the interest of saving time, the authority to make the declaration has been delegated to the Federal Emergency Management Agency's (FEMA's) Regional Administrators. Once issued, the FMAG declaration authorizes various forms of federal fire suppression assistance such as the provision of equipment, personnel, and grants to state, local, and tribal governments for the control, management, and mitigation of any fire on certain public or private forest land or grassland that might become a major disaster. This federal assistance requires a cost-sharing component such that state, local, and tribal governments are responsible for 25% of the expenses. This report answers frequently asked questions about FMAGs. This report will be updated as events warrant."
  ],
